<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#prince-of-transylvania">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prince of Transylvania</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1570">1570</a>–<a href="/years/1711">1711</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> was the title of the rulers of the Principality of Transylvania, first taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-sigismund-zapolya">John Sigismund Zápolya</a> when he gave up the Hungarian royal title at the Treaty of Speyer in 1570. The princes were elected by the Transylvanian diet and ruled under Ottoman suzerainty until the principality passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> at the end of the 17th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prince-of-transylvania.jpg" alt="Prince of Transylvania" width="320" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dahn · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_Transylvania_in_Cod._icon._391.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> was governed by a voivode appointed by the king, who presided over the assemblies of its seven counties. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szekelys">Székelys</a> had their own institutions, but from the middle of the 15th century the voivodes were also appointed Count of the Székelys. In 1437, during the Budai Nagy Antal Revolt, the nobles, Saxons and Székelys formed an alliance, confirmed in 1459 as the union of the Three Nations of Transylvania.</p><p>After the Ottoman victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Battle of Mohács</a> in 1526, Archduke Ferdinand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-zapolya">John Zápolya</a> both claimed the Hungarian throne, and John sought the support of Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a>. His infant son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-sigismund-zapolya">John Sigismund Zápolya</a>, was elected king as John II Sigismund in 1540; Suleiman placed central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> under direct Ottoman rule and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> and eastern Hungary to him. John Sigismund called himself elected king until 1570, when he renounced the royal title and adopted the style Prince of Transylvania and Lord of parts of Hungary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>His successor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-bathory">Stephen Báthory</a>, at first styled himself voivode, as the royal governors had done, and took the princely title only on his election as King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1576, from when its use became stable. On his death in 1586 it passed to his nephew Sigismund Báthory, and Emperor Rudolph II confirmed the style on 28 January 1595. The full style ran: by the grace of God, Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, Lord of parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and Count of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szekelys">Székelys</a>.</p><p>The princes were not recognised as independent monarchs, acknowledging at times Ottoman suzerainty and at other times the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>. In Hanafi Islamic law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> belonged to the House of Agreement, between the Ottoman lands proper and independent states. Each new prince received from the sultan a document, an ahidnâme, which confirmed the estates' right to elect him freely and promised military help, in return for a yearly tribute and support in Ottoman campaigns. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rakoczis-war-of-independence">Rákóczi's War of Independence</a> the princes were in effect replaced by governors, and the last prince, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii-rakoczi">Francis II Rákóczi</a>, spent the rest of his life in exile.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Prince of Transylvania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Speyer in 1570, John Sigismund gave up the title of king and was recognised as <b>Prince of Transylvania</b>, turning the realm into the Principality of Transylvania.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-hungarian-kingdom">Eastern Hungarian Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Speyer in 1570, John Sigismund Zápolya renounced the Hungarian royal title and became <b>Prince of Transylvania</b>, ruling Transylvania and the Partium, eastern Hungarian counties, under Ottoman suzerainty with his court at Gyulafehérvár.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-transylvania">Principality of Transylvania</a></li></ul></section>
